                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
                  %h2#theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu%27s+theorem"} Diaconescu's theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr%27s+theorem"} Barr's theorem
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/topos+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+subtopos"} essential subtopos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mi l
                  %mo ↪
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_l \hookrightarrow \mathbf{H}
            is called a
            %em level
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            of an
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+cylinder"} adjoint cylinder
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
          %p
            The terminology is due to the fact (
            %a{:href => "#KL89"}> Kelly-Lawvere 89
            ) that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtoposes"} subtoposes
            of a topos, or more generally the essential localizations of a suitably complete category, form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete lattice
          %p
            If for two levels
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{1} \hookrightarrow \mathbf{H}_2
            the second one includes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
            of the first one, and if it is minimal with this property, then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
            speaks of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"}> Aufhebung
            ” (see there for details) of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            exhibited by the first one.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subtopos"} open subtopos
                is a level.
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
                the topos of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
                = succeed "-inclusions" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+coskeleton"} n-coskeleton
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                is a sequence of levels (the extra left adjoint exhibiting the essentialness being the
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+skeleton"} simplicial skeleton
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
                in this example is discussed in detail in (
                %a{:href => "#KRRZ11"}> KRRZ 11
                ).
            %li
              %p
                The lowest level is the inclusion of the trivial subtopos as the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
                the section
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/adjoint+modality#Werden"} Werden
            %li
              %p
                Often the level above this will be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
                and the level above that, if it exists,
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
              %p
                Here differential cohesion itself typically comes in a countable tower of levels, given by the
                = succeed "th" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+objects"} infinitesimal objects
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegelian+taco"} Hegelian taco
                abstracts a certain stacking of three levels viewed as a diagram in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                into a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphic+monoid"} graphic monoid
            %li
              %p
                An essential localization
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mo !
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_!\dashv i^\ast \dashv i_\ast :\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B}
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mo !
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_!\simeq i_\ast
                is called a
                %strong quintessential localization
                (cf.
                %a{:href => "#JS96"} Johnstone 1996
                and the discussion at
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quality+type"} quality type
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The lattice of essential localizations of a category is contained in the lattice of all localizations. While the suprema of localizations coincide in both lattices, the infinimum in the lattice of all localizations of even a pair of essential localizations need
            %em not
            be an essential localization (
            %a{:href => "#KL89"}> Kelly-Lawvere 89
            ).
          %p
            Recall (e.g. from
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Borceux1"} Borceux 1994
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunctions
            compose: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\dashv F:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi H
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\dashv H:\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{C}
            then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi K
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\circ K \dashv H\circ F:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{C}
          #levelscompose.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Given two essential localizations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_!\dashv i^\ast \dashv i_\ast :\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!\dashv q^\ast \dashv q_\ast :\mathcal{B}\to\mathcal{C}
              , hence in the situation:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mo ↪
                      %mo ↪
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mo ↪
                      %mo ↪
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{A}\overset{\hookrightarrow}{\underset{\hookrightarrow}{\to}} \mathcal{B}\overset{\hookrightarrow}{\underset{\hookrightarrow}{\to}}\mathcal{C}
            %p
              there results a composed essential localization
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mo !
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mo !
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msup
                        %mi q
                        %mo *
                      %mo ⊣
                      %msub
                        %mi q
                        %mo *
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!\cdot i_!\dashv i^\ast \cdot q^\ast \dashv q_\ast\cdot i_\ast :\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            Here , of course,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^\ast \cdot q^\ast \dashv q_\ast\cdot i_\ast
            is just the adjunction involved in the composition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\cdot i
            of the two geometric morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            (provided the categories involved are toposes), the important thing is that the essentialities
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_!
            compose as well.
          %p Since this just states that inclusions as well as essential geometric morphism are closed under composition and therefore so are essential inclusions, our primary interest here is to extract an explicit formula for the modalities corresponding to the composition:
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modalities"} adjoint modalities
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (q_!\cdot i_!)\cdot (i^\ast \cdot q^\ast) \dashv  (q_\ast\cdot i_\ast)\cdot  (i^\ast \cdot q^\ast) :\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{C}
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+topology"} rigid topology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quality+type"} quality type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtopos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subtopos"} open subtopos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+sublocale"} essential sublocale
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+subtopos"} essential subtopos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential geometric morphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegelian+taco"} Hegelian taco
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#SGA4
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Artin"}> M.Artin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} A.Grothendieck
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+L.+Verdier"} J. L. Verdier
                (eds.),
                %em Théorie des Topos et Cohomologie Etale des Schémas - SGA 4
                , Springer LNM
                %strong 269
                Heidelberg 1972. (sec. IV 7.6., pp.414-416)
            %li#Borceux1
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> F. Borceux
                ,
                %em Handbook of Categorical Algebra vol. 1
                , Cambridge UP 1994.
            %li#JS96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> P. Johnstone
                ,
                %em Remarks on Quintessential and Persistent Localizations
                , TAC
                %strong 2
                no.8 (1996) pp.90-99. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/1996/n8/n8.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#KL89
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+M.+Kelly"}> G. M. Kelly
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"} F. W. Lawvere
                %em On the Complete Lattice of Essential Localizations
                , Bull.Soc.Math. de Belgique
                %strong XLI
                (1989) 289-319 &amp;lbrack;
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Kelly-Lawvere_EssentialLocalizations.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#KRRZ11
              %p
                C. Kennett,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"} E. Riehl
                %em Levels in the toposes of simplicial sets and cubical sets
                , JPAA
                %strong 215
                no.5 (2011) pp.949-961. (
